{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.DilogPartARiemannSum","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Archimedean.DilogPartARiemannSum","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.DilogarithmArchimedeanLimit"],"declarations":4,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.tendsto_pow_mul_nhds_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.tendsto_pow_mul_nhds_zero","kind":"theorem","line":106,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartARiemannSum.lean#L106-L116","doc":"**m-series termwise-limit ingredient (2) — the tail factor vanishes.**  For `0 < q < 1` the numerator\ntail `q^{(m+1)n} → 0` as `n → ∞` (`= (q^{m+1})ⁿ` with `0 < q^{m+1} < 1`).  This\nis the `n → ∞` half of the `q^{(m+1)n} → 0` behaviour that, jointly with\n`n(1-q) → ∞`, sends the numerator `(q^{(m+1)n} − 1) → −1`; combined with\nBrick 3 the `m`-th term tends to the Riemann weight `x^{m+1}/(m+1)²`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.tendsto_one_sub_div_pow_sub_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.tendsto_one_sub_div_pow_sub_one","kind":"theorem","line":76,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartARiemannSum.lean#L76-L104","doc":"**m-series termwise-limit ingredient (1) — the per-term coefficient limit.**\n(Independent of the leading Riemann-sum route in `DilogPartAConvergence.lean`\n/ PR #4106; this is the parallel `m`-series route.)  In the `m`-series form\nof `(1-q)·log Φ_n` (`partialProduct_log_eq_mseries`), the `q`-dependent\nrescaling of the `m`-th term is `(1-q)/(q^{m+1}-1)`, and it tends to the\nRiemann weight `-1/(m+1)` as `q → 1`.  Proof: for `q ≠ 1`,\n`q^{m+1}-1 = (q-1)·Σ_{j≤m} qʲ` (`geom_sum_eq`), so the ratio equals\n`-1/Σ_{j≤m} qʲ`, which is continuous at `q = 1` with value `-1/(m+1)`.\nCombined with `qⁿ⁽ᵐ⁺¹⁾ → 0` (when `n(1-q) → ∞`) this gives the termwise limit\n`x^{m+1}/(m+1)²`; the dominated-convergence exchange with the `m`-sum is the\nremaining core (still open). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.riemann_sum_form","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.riemann_sum_form","kind":"theorem","line":56,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartARiemannSum.lean#L56-L74","doc":"**Brick 2 — the Riemann-sum structure.**  The rescaled sum\n`(1-q)·Σ_{k<n} −log(1 - x qᵏ)` is *exactly* the left-endpoint Riemann sum of\nthe integrand `h(u) = −log(1 - x u)/u` over the geometric partition\n`1 = q⁰ > q¹ > ⋯ > qⁿ`, whose `k`-th cell has width `qᵏ − qᵏ⁺¹ = qᵏ(1-q)`:\n\n`(1-q)·Σ_{k<n} −log(1 - x qᵏ) = Σ_{k<n} h(qᵏ)·(qᵏ − qᵏ⁺¹)`.\n\nAs `q → 1⁻` (mesh → 0) with `qⁿ → 0` (the partition fills `(0,1]`), this\nRiemann sum converges to `∫₀¹ −log(1 - x u)/u du = Li₂(x)` — the remaining\nanalytic core of Part A. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QPochhammer.log_partialProduct_eq_neg_sum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QPochhammer.log_partialProduct_eq_neg_sum","kind":"theorem","line":39,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/DilogPartARiemannSum.lean#L39-L54","doc":"**Brick 1 — the Riemann-sum reduction.**  For `0 < x < 1` and `0 < q ≤ 1`\nevery factor `1 - x qᵏ` is positive, so the rescaled logarithm of the partial\nproduct is a finite sum:  `log Φ_n(x;q) = −Σ_{k<n} log(1 - x qᵏ)`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}